# Reset the Logitech C920's exposure_dynamic_framerate control to 0 on
# every (re)connect.
#
# The camera ships with this control ON (=1) and a replug resets it back
# to 1 even if it was previously turned off in software -- with it on, the
# camera lengthens its shutter (instead of raising gain) in dim lighting,
# which silently drops the actual output below the requested publish rate
# (e.g. 30fps -> 15fps) in a way that looks identical to a USB bandwidth
# problem. camera_driver.py also tries to clear this at open() time, but
# that only covers this node's own process; this rule covers manual
# v4l2-ctl/other-tool usage and any camera never unplugged since boot.
#
# idVendor 046d matches the by-id device path already used in
# autodrive_bringup/config/camera.yaml. idProduct 082d is the standard C920
# ID -- verify with `lsusb | grep -i logitech` on the actual vehicle if a
# camera doesn't respond to this rule (`udevadm test` or check
# /var/log/syslog for the RUN command's exit status).
#
# ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES restricts this to the actual video-capture node --
# a UVC device like the C920 also exposes a metadata-only node that
# doesn't support this control and would just fail the RUN command.
#
# Install (not wired into colcon's install/ tree -- udev doesn't look
# there):
#   sudo install -m 644 src/autodrive/autodrive_bringup/config/udev/99-c920-exposure.rules \
#     /etc/udev/rules.d/99-c920-exposure.rules
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#   sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=video4linux

SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTRS{idVendor}=="046d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="082d", \
  ENV{ID_V4L_CAPABILITIES}=="*:capture:*", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/v4l2-ctl -d $devnode --set-ctrl exposure_dynamic_framerate=0"
